<p>Air travel across much of the Middle East has been thrown into disarray after U.S. and Israeli strikes on Iran, followed by an escalation that triggered widespread airspace closures and airport shutdowns. The result: hundreds of thousands of passengers scrambling for new routes and workable plans. Major transit hubs including Dubai, Doha and Abu Dhabi suspended or scaled back operations, setting off mass cancellations and diversions that quickly spilled into flight schedules worldwide. For travelers, the disruption has been both logistical and personal. Airline phone lines jammed, connections were missed, and waits stretched on as carriers paused services and worked through rebookings around closed airspace. Some businesses in the region also shifted staff to working from home as the disruption continued. The situation is still changing. Airlines and airports are posting rolling updates and, in some cases, offering more flexible rebooking or refund options—small but real relief for people stuck far from where they meant to be, while the region’s flight paths remain uncertain.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/gNuAXH3KQZGCnrVdDbXt5neoM_ncSxIHmqfskQ_GckT-WYuhnZ_JCjL0CJpdl0qymErtEm-J5iODMA8Ln6dDcuLtOeaGjhhb-LXlO_13zBkTDrHh2XXSBsN999Oaa8qe41ZVcPkBHnMfOX_Z2HGWNYVbWjjc_qHz4CkOg0GDLJKo0eA9L5M3lGv30uzcCnnhDwSoF923nxetx4nwoK3dON2_xjjRm-hCYO0e9fbS1YanNQ' alt='Passengers wait as airlines adjust schedules amid Middle East airspace disruptions.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Cancellation count: FlightAware counted more than 2,400 flight cancellations on Sunday across airports in the Middle East.</li><li>Operational pauses: The Guardian reported Emirates paused all planned services to and from Dubai until 3pm UAE time on Monday, Etihad paused flights to and from Abu Dhabi until 2pm UAE time, and Qatar Airways suspended operations due to the closure of Qatari airspace.</li><li>Airline flexibility: Cathay Pacific paused Hong Kong–Dubai flights until Thursday and Hong Kong–Riyadh flights until Tuesday, and is offering rebooking, rerouting or refunds with fees waived for affected tickets.</li><li>Market spillover: Airline shares in Asia fell sharply as investors weighed higher fuel costs, rerouting and cancellations, with Qantas down as much as 10.4% early Monday and several carriers down more than 5%.</li><li>Energy shock: Oil prices jumped about 7% to their highest in months as attacks and disruption in the region raised concerns about shipments from a major producing area.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Gulf-carrier updates (The Guardian): Emirates, Etihad and Qatar Airways announced service suspensions tied to Dubai, Abu Dhabi and Qatari airspace restrictions, publishing specific local-time windows for halted operations as the situation changed. (<a href='https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/mar/02/flights-cancelled-middle-east-travel-chaos-us-israeli-iran-conflictflights-cancelled-middle-east-travel-chaos-us-israeli-iran-conflict'>The Guardian</a>)</li><li>Iran-linked framing (RT): RT called the U.S.-Israeli strikes unprovoked and said Iran retaliated and reportedly targeted regional aviation hubs, followed by airspace closures announced across multiple countries. (<a href='https://www.rt.com/news/633328-iran-airspace-chaos-stranded/'>RT</a>)</li><li>US coverage focus (CBS News): CBS reported that the airstrikes stranded thousands of Americans traveling to or through the Middle East. (<a href='https://www.cbsnews.com/video/americans-stranded-in-the-middle-east-amid-strikes/'>CBS News</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.pbs.org/newshour/world/hundreds-of-thousands-of-travelers-stranded-following-u-s-israel-attacks-on-iran'>Hundreds of thousands of travelers stranded following U.S.-Israel attacks on Iran</a> - pbs.org</li><li><a href='https://michaelwest.com.au/airline-shares-fall-as-us-iran-conflict-disrupts-travel/'>Airline shares fall as US-Iran conflict disrupts travel</a> - michaelwest.com